<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Pawmodoro — app privacy manifest.

    This file is a legal declaration. Every key in it was put here because a
    grep of Pawmodoro/ found the API, at a line number written down in
    docs/MAC_APP_STORE.md § 7. Nothing here is aspirational and nothing is
    copied from a template. If you add a key, be able to name the file and
    line it is true of; if you delete code, come back and delete the key.

    The five required-reason categories, and what the audit found:

      File timestamp    — NOT USED. The only FileManager calls are
                          Model/Snapshot.swift:107-177: urls(for:in:),
                          fileExists, createDirectory, contentsOfDirectory
                          (atPath: — names only, no attribute keys),
                          removeItem. None of them reads a timestamp.
      System boot time  — NOT USED. ProcessInfo is touched only for
                          .arguments (the debug launch flags). No
                          systemUptime, no mach_absolute_time.
      Disk space        — NOT USED. No statfs, no volumeAvailableCapacity*.
      Active keyboards  — NOT USED. No activeInputModes; the app has one
                          text field and does not care what is typing into it.
      User defaults     — USED. Declared below.

    Two reasons are declared for user defaults, not one, and the second is
    the one that is easy to get wrong:

      CA92.1  — the app's own container. UserDefaults.standard, in about
                thirty model types (Model/*.swift), holding settings, the
                session history, the journal, the bond and every other store.
                Nothing in there is read by anything but this app.

      1C8F.1  — the App Group container. LiveActivity/WidgetMirror.swift:70
                opens UserDefaults(suiteName: "group.com.pawmodoro") and
                writes two strings that the home-screen widget extension
                reads back. CA92.1 explicitly does NOT cover that: it says
                it "does not permit ... writing information that can be
                accessed by other apps". Declaring CA92.1 alone would be an
                under-declaration, which is the same kind of untrue as an
                over-declaration.

    NSPrivacyTracking is false, the tracking-domain list is empty and the
    collected-data list is empty, and those three are the same fact stated
    three ways: this app makes no network calls at all. Zero hits for
    URLSession, NWConnection, WKWebView or an http(s) literal across every
    .swift file in the project. docs/PRIVACY.md says so out loud, and that
    sentence is load-bearing.

    Scope: Apple applies required-reason API rules to iOS, iPadOS, tvOS,
    visionOS and watchOS — macOS is not on that list. This is one
    multiplatform target, so the Mac build carries the manifest too. That is
    harmless and it is also true there, so there is nothing to condition.

    The widget extension is a separate bundle and needs its own manifest;
    it has one at PawmodoroWidgets/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
				<string>1C8F.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
